    "reply": "The search retrieved thirty BRCA1 missense variants from ClinVar, all named on transcript NM_007294.4, though one record lacks a complete HGVS expression in the returned data. These entries provide the primary variant identifiers and protein-level annotations used in clinical classification workflows.\n\n## Variant Identifiers and Nomenclature\n\nEach finding reports a ClinVar variant accession paired with an HGVS cDNA and protein change on the canonical BRCA1 transcript. Examples include ClinVar:1000183, name NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4709T>C (p.Leu1570Pro) [1], ClinVar:1000551, name NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.3664G>A (p.Glu1222Lys) [2], and ClinVar:1001081, name NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.353T>C (p.Leu118Pro) [3]. The full set spans residues across the BRCA1 protein sequence, from p.Asp214Val to p.Pro1614Ala [26][30]. One entry, ClinVar:1010631, returned only the accession without accompanying HGVS detail [29].\n\n## Coverage Across Protein Domains\n\nThe variants distribute throughout the BRCA1 coding sequence. N-terminal examples include p.Leu118Pro [3] and p.Lys260Glu [20], while C-terminal entries include p.Pro1614Ala [30] and p.Pro1562Arg [12]. Mid-gene positions include p.Gln1388Lys [16] and p.Arg1443Leu [7]. This breadth indicates that the retrieved set captures missense changes across multiple functional regions of the protein.\n\n## Assembly and Transcript Context\n\nAll named variants use the same transcript reference, NM_007294.4, which provides consistent cDNA coordinates for classification comparison. The findings do not specify genomic assembly coordinates or SPDI expressions; researchers requiring GRCh37 or GRCh38 context would need to map these transcript-level annotations to genomic positions through additional lookup.",
